# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# parasite-monster-frogs (Miya's Note). Script from miya 2026-10-09 (reworked 2026-10-24 spec; fact-checked and rewritten 2026-10-29, see /mnt/project-files/parasite-monster-frogs/script-check.md).
SCENES = [
 ('S01', 'A Quiet Pond', 'ill', 'p1', 'Imagine walking along the edge of a serene pond on a warm summer afternoon. The water is calm, dragonflies drift through the reeds, and crickets chirp in the fresh air. Everything looks like untouched paradise. But beneath the calm surface, a hidden war of survival is unfolding. A tiny organism is preparing to turn innocent amphibians into creatures out of a science fiction horror film.'),
 ('S02', 'The Minnesota Frogs', 'ill', 'p2', 'Our story begins in the summer of 1995 in rural Minnesota. A group of students on a school field trip made a discovery that shocked the scientific community. As they dipped their nets into the shallow marsh water, they did not pull out ordinary northern leopard frogs. They caught amphibians with twisted bodies, missing limbs, and, in the most terrifying cases, extra legs growing out of their abdomens.'),
 ('S03', 'Henderson Field Trip', 'ill', 'p3', 'The students were on a farm near the town of Henderson, in south-central Minnesota, and their teacher, Cindy Reinitz, quickly realized this was no ordinary school trip. Their discovery sent state scientists to the site. According to reports of the follow-up survey, a large share of the frogs collected there were deformed. That was far beyond anything anyone expected to see in a quiet farm pond.'),
 ('S04', 'Too Many Legs', 'ill', 'p4', 'Some of these frogs had several extra legs sprouting from the pelvic region. Others had no hind legs at all and had to crawl painfully on their tiny front arms. A few were found with legs growing backwards, or jutting out of the chest. It looked as though nature had suffered a glitch and spawned real-life monsters.'),
 ('S05', 'The World Reacts', 'ill', 'p5', 'Within weeks, news of the multi-legged frogs spread far beyond Minnesota. Alarm grew among wildlife biologists, local communities, and health agencies. People naturally wondered if the water itself had been poisoned. If frogs in local water were mutating this badly, what might be waiting for the people who drank from the same sources?'),
 ('S06', 'The Pollution Suspects', 'ill', 'p6', 'The first suspect was human pollution. Frogs have thin, permeable skin that absorbs water and air directly from their surroundings, which makes them famous warning signs of environmental trouble. The suspect list included agricultural pesticides, radioactive waste, heavy metals, and thinning ozone letting in more ultraviolet radiation. Many people assumed that modern society had poisoned the swamps with artificial chemicals.'),
 ('S07', 'The Lab Dead End', 'ill', 'p7', 'Funding followed, and so did laboratory tests. Scientists exposed healthy tadpoles to high concentrations of various pesticides and intense ultraviolet radiation. Some chemicals did cause deformities, stunted growth, or sudden death. But none of them reproduced the hyper-specific, multi-legged pattern seen in nature. The chemical hypothesis had hit a frustrating dead end.'),
 ('S08', 'Into the Mud', 'ill', 'p8', "A year after the Minnesota story hit the news, a young researcher named Pieter Johnson went looking in a different place. Instead of staying in a laboratory, he went out to the ponds of Santa Clara County, California, where deformed frogs had also been found. He surveyed about thirty ponds, comparing the water, the animals, and the exact places where deformed frogs appeared. His field work was about to uncover one of nature's darkest secrets."),
 ('S09', 'The Snail Clue', 'ill', 'p9', 'The pattern was clear. Deformed amphibians turned up only in ponds that also held a certain kind of snail. And when researchers examined the deformed frogs closely, they found clusters of tiny cysts packed around the limb buds and the pelvic region. Inside each cyst lived a microscopic parasitic flatworm, known scientifically as Ribeiroia ondatrae. The culprit behind the multi-legged monsters was not a chemical spill or nuclear waste. It was a parasite.'),
 ('S10', 'Not a Chemical', 'ill', 'p10', 'That was a powerful clue. It meant the cause was not simply something in the water. It was something that needed snails. A chemical spill does not need a snail to do its damage. A parasite with a snail in its life cycle does.'),
 ('S11', 'Three Hosts', 'ill', 'p11', 'To understand how a worm this small accomplishes such a feat, we have to look at its life cycle. Ribeiroia ondatrae is a trematode, a type of flatworm that cannot complete its life inside one animal. It needs three different hosts from different animal groups. Its journey begins in a humble creature at the bottom of the pond: the freshwater ramshorn snail.'),
 ('S12', 'Inside the Snail', 'ill', 'p12', "When a microscopic swimming larva called a miracidium finds a ramshorn snail, it burrows into the snail's soft tissue. Once inside, it behaves like an invading army. It feeds on the snail's reproductive tissue and sterilizes the snail, diverting the snail's energy into feeding the parasite. Inside this living factory, the flatworm multiplies into huge numbers of clones."),
 ('S13', 'Swimming Cercariae', 'ill', 'p13', 'After some weeks, the clones mature into a free-swimming stage called cercariae. A single infected snail can release large numbers of these microscopic, fork-tailed swimmers into the water. Cercariae do not feed, and their energy reserves are limited. They are generally thought to survive only a day or two, so they have one desperate mission: find the second host.'),
 ('S14', 'The Tadpole Target', 'ill', 'p14', 'Their target is a developing tadpole. At this delicate stage, small clusters of cells called limb buds are forming near the base of the tail. These buds hold the instructions for growing two perfectly matched hind legs. And the cercariae go straight to this spot.'),
 ('S15', 'Timing Is Everything', 'ill', 'p15', 'Timing is everything. In a study of leopard frogs, tadpoles infected before their limb buds had formed often died, while tadpoles infected at the limb bud stage tended to survive with malformations. In other words, the parasite does its most precise damage at one fragile moment. Cercariae can also infect fish, where they settle along the head and gills.'),
 ('S16', 'The Invasion', 'ill', 'p16', 'When a cercaria reaches a tadpole, it attacks the skin. It sheds its swimming tail, pushes its small body through the tissue, and burrows toward the limb bud. For the tadpole, this invasion is like being struck by microscopic drill bits.'),
 ('S17', 'The Armored Cyst', 'ill', 'p17', 'Once settled deep inside the limb-forming tissue, the parasite builds a thick wall around itself. It becomes an immobile, armored cyst called a metacercaria. These cysts act like physical barricades inside the delicate flesh. But the damage is not only a matter of blockage. The cysts also upset the way the limb is built.'),
 ('S18', 'A Scrambled Plan', 'ill', 'p18', 'Limbs do not simply grow at random. Their development is guided by chemical signals, including a vitamin A derivative called retinoic acid. Cells read these signals to decide whether to build a thigh, a knee, a foot, or toes. Researchers have found changes in these signals in infected limb buds, but the exact way the parasite scrambles the plan is still debated. What is clear is that the cysts disturb the growing tissue.'),
 ('S19', 'Extra Legs', 'ill', 'p19', 'When the building plan is scrambled, the result can be chaos. Instead of growing a single leg on each side, the tissue can start the leg-building process more than once. Extra, fully formed limbs may emerge from the same hip joint.'),
 ('S20', 'Missing Legs', 'ill', 'p20', 'The outcome depends heavily on how many parasites attack and where they land. Heavy disruption can cause extra limbs. In other cases, limbs can be missing, shortened to stumps, or fused together. Skin webs can even trap the legs against the body.'),
 ('S21', 'The Crippled Frog', 'ill', 'p21', 'When the tadpole finishes its metamorphosis and crawls onto land, it is badly crippled. A normal frog relies on powerful hind legs to leap away from danger, dive into water, and catch fast insects. A frog with tangled, useless legs cannot jump properly. Every attempt ends in clumsy flailing, and moving even a few inches costs enormous effort.'),
 ('S22', 'Why Cripple It?', 'ill', 'p22', 'At this point, any careful observer must ask a fundamental question: why? Parasites usually do best when their hosts stay alive and healthy. Crippling a host seems counterproductive and needlessly cruel. Why would Ribeiroia ondatrae spend so much effort turning a frog into a helpless monster? The answer lies at the end of its life cycle.'),
 ('S23', 'The Final Host', 'ill', 'p23', "Look up from the water, into the sky. Above the reeds glides a predator: a heron, an egret, or a hunting duck. Waterbirds like these are final hosts in the flatworm's life cycle. Only inside a bird's gut can the adult parasite mature and reproduce. But a microscopic worm in a muddy pond cannot simply fly up and infect a bird."),
 ('S24', 'The Bridge', 'ill', 'p24', 'Here is the sinister genius of evolution, a phenomenon scientists call parasite-induced trophic transmission. To get from a swimming tadpole into the warm gut of a bird, the parasite needs a bridge, and that bridge is predation. A fast, healthy frog will spot a hunting heron, leap into deep water, and escape. But a crippled frog is a sitting duck on the shoreline. Researchers argue that this is no accident. Deformed hosts are easier to catch, and that helps the parasite move on.'),
 ('S25', 'The Heron Strikes', 'ill', 'p25', "When a heron spots a deformed frog thrashing clumsily in the shallows, the outcome is quick. The bird's spear-like beak strikes down and it swallows the frog whole. The frog's life ends. But for the parasites encysted in its distorted legs, the plan has worked."),
 ('S26', 'Inside the Bird', 'ill', 'p26', "In the bird's stomach, the cyst walls dissolve and the parasites are set free. The flatworms attach to the intestinal wall, mature into adults, and produce eggs. The eggs leave the bird in its droppings, ready to be dropped into the world."),
 ('S27', 'The Circle Closes', 'ill', 'p27', 'The bird flies on, perhaps for many miles, and drops its droppings into new ponds and wetlands. When the eggs hit the water, they hatch into miracidia, which immediately begin hunting for ramshorn snails. The circle is complete. Through evolution alone, a brainless flatworm has manipulated the body of a vertebrate to steer its own journey across the sky.'),
 ('S28', 'An Old Relationship', 'ill', 'p28', 'This parasite is not a modern invention. Scientists who studied old museum specimens found malformed frogs with Ribeiroia infection going back to at least the 1940s. So this relationship is old. Why, then, did the 1990s bring such a startling wave of deformities? Why did sites that had been quiet suddenly fill with monsters?'),
 ('S29', 'The 1946 Frogs', 'ill', 'p29', 'Researchers resurveyed eight sites where malformed frogs had been collected in the past. At six of those sites, the old malformations were linked to Ribeiroia, and the oldest case dated back to 1946. At one site, nine old specimens carried forty extra hind limbs between them. The parasite had been shaping frogs long before the headlines.'),
 ('S30', 'Humans Re-Enter', 'ill', 'p30', 'This is where human civilization re-enters the picture. Johnson and his colleagues found a link between nutrient pollution and the severity of the infections. Farmers use large amounts of fertilizer containing nitrogen and phosphorus to boost crop yields. When heavy rain washes the surplus into nearby wetlands, it sets off a chain reaction.'),
 ('S31', 'Fertilizer and Algae', 'ill', 'p31', 'The extra nutrients feed blooms of green algae that blanket the pond surface in thick green sludge. For ramshorn snails, this algae is an all-you-can-eat buffet. Snail populations in nutrient-rich ponds can grow sharply. More snails mean more homes for Ribeiroia, and ordinary ponds can turn into parasite-breeding superfactories.'),
 ('S32', 'The 2007 Study', 'ill', 'p32', 'A later study by Johnson and his colleagues, published in 2007, tested exactly this idea. It found that nutrient enrichment raised the number of snails, and that those snails then released more infectious parasites into the water. An earlier study had found that nutrient pollution also changes which snail species dominate a pond, and one of the winners is the very kind of snail that Ribeiroia prefers.'),
 ('S33', 'Parasite Superfactory', 'ill', 'p33', 'In a balanced pond, a tadpole might meet only a few cercariae, and the effects may be minor. In a nutrient-rich pond, a tadpole can be attacked by many parasites at once, and its developing body is overwhelmed. What was once a rare curiosity can become an epidemic of monsters.'),
 ('S34', 'Honest Limits', 'ill', 'p34', 'It is worth being honest about the limits of this story. Ribeiroia explains the multi-legged frogs, but it is not the only threat amphibians face. Habitat loss, disease, and other factors also harm frogs around the world, and not every deformity in every pond has the same cause. Nature rarely offers a single villain.'),
 ('S35', 'The Web of Life', 'ill', 'p35', 'The mystery of the multi-legged frog teaches a humbling lesson. We cannot isolate one creature from the web that surrounds it. What looked like a simple case of toxic dumping turned out to be an intricate chain linking farmers, fertilizer, algae, snails, flatworms, tadpoles, and birds. Every small change we introduce ripples outward in ways we cannot predict.'),
 ('S36', 'A Triumph of Evolution', 'ill', 'p36', 'Parasites like Ribeiroia are often viewed with disgust, and understandably so. Their strategies seem cruel to our human morality. Yet from a purely evolutionary view, they are an astonishing triumph. Without eyes, without a brain, and without complex organs, this tiny worm has evolved the chemistry to reshape the flesh and bone of a far larger animal, all to ensure its own survival.'),
 ('S37', 'The Hidden Battlefield', 'ill', 'p37', 'Next time you stand beside a quiet pond, do not be fooled by the glassy, peaceful reflection. Beneath the surface lies a hidden battlefield, shaped by millions of years of evolutionary warfare. The natural world is stranger, darker, and far more connected than we imagine.'),
]
